Summary

Records completion of the Phase 6 performance audit in the authoritative portfolio rebuild plan.

Changes

Updated docs/PORTFOLIO_REBUILD_PLAN.md to:

mark Performance audit complete;
record Lighthouse 13.0.1 mobile and desktop Performance scores of 100;
summarize the measured FCP, LCP, Speed Index, Total Blocking Time, and CLS results;
record that the production page ships no client-side JavaScript, Astro hydration, external fonts, analytics, tracking, or third-party requests;
note the small normal page-load request count and transferred size;
confirm that no meaningful performance defect or regression was found;
defer the optional MediCheck image optimization to future image-related work;
record GitHub Pages cache behavior as contextual hosting behavior rather than a deployment defect;
note that the audit used controlled lab measurements because real-user field data was unavailable;
set Cross-browser review as the only remaining Phase 6 checklist item;
set the next focus to one read-only Phase 6 cross-browser review;
add the related decision-log entry.
Validation
npm.cmd run check — passed with 0 errors, 0 warnings, and 0 hints
npm.cmd run build — passed
git diff --check — passed
Scope confirmation

Only this file changed:

docs/PORTFOLIO_REBUILD_PLAN.md

No application source, styles, components, assets, dependencies, deployment files, generated output, legacy files, résumé files, or metadata assets were changed.

## What changed

Connects the already-frozen cross-geometry ALIS/VROOM proposal to the reusable matrix executor without changing the proposal itself:

- a dedicated one-purpose authorization guard bound to the exact proposal, contract, validator, adapters, workflow, runtime lock, plan, executor, aggregate, audit, and screening driver;
- transformation of the 24 proposal cases into the generic `mystic-batch-v1` matrix plan;
- a runtime-aware adapter that preserves the frozen ALIS/VROOM input distinction;
- a manual-only matrix workflow with `fail-fast: false`, maximum six parallel cases, one syntax check and at most one solver execution per case, and no retry;
- generic aggregation and independent audit followed by the frozen cross-geometry screening analysis;
- seven tests covering one-purpose authorization, multi-file refusal, exact planning, runtime mismatch refusal, and audited screening analysis.

## Root gap

The proposal package deliberately remained non-executable, while the generic executor accepted a different manifest header and adapter interface. This bridge preserves both contracts rather than weakening either one.

## Safety boundary

The active authorization is byte-for-byte identical to the disabled template. This PR does not authorize or dispatch MYSTIC, run a syntax check, or execute a solver. A future run requires a separate one-purpose commit changing only `authorization.cross-geometry.json`, followed by a reviewed first-attempt `workflow_dispatch`.

Even a completed run is screening evidence only and does not establish all-geometry agreement, final engine validity, surrogate readiness, observational validity, or production permission.

## Validation

Local validation passed:

- 7/7 execution-bridge tests;
- Python compilation of all four bridge modules;
- YAML parsing of both workflows;
- exact 10-file diff from current `main`;
- active authorization remains disabled.
